    "q-143899": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143899",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}, & 1<x<e \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{1}{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$",
      "worked_solution": "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\frac{1}{X}.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y})\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{y})}{(\\frac{1}{y})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $1<x<e,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b=e,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=1,\\,u(e)={e}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{y}, & {e}^{-1}<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
